# ChatHub (lqdflying/chathub)

Custom fork/distribution of LobeChat, maintained independently starting at v3.0.0 (rebranded to ChatHub at v1.0.0). Targets **self-hosted Docker + PostgreSQL only**. Docker image: `docker.io/lqdflying/chathub`.

Upstream: https://github.com/lobehub/lobe-chat — diverged significantly. **Do not blindly sync upstream changes.**

## Tech Stack

- **Frontend**: Next.js 15, React 19, TypeScript — hybrid Next.js App Router + SPA (`react-router-dom`)
- **UI**: `@lobehub/ui`, Ant Design, `antd-style` (CSS-in-JS via `createStyles`)
- **State**: Zustand stores + SWR for server data
- **Backend**: tRPC + Next.js API routes
- **Database**: PostgreSQL only (server mode), Drizzle ORM
- **Testing**: Vitest + Testing Library (unit), Playwright + Cucumber (e2e)
- **Monorepo**: pnpm workspaces; use `bun` for scripts, `bunx` for executables

## DB migrations — safety net (REQUIRED)

Every new Drizzle migration that adds a **table** or **column** MUST also add a corresponding `ensure*` call in `scripts/migrateServerDB/docker.cjs`. This protects against **journal drift** — deployments where `__drizzle_migrations` records the migration as applied but the actual DDL never executed (failed build, restored DB backup, partial transaction).

**Pattern:**
1. Create the migration SQL (`bun run db:generate`) — Drizzle already uses `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`
2. Create a `scripts/migrateServerDB/ensure<Name>.cjs` file (see `ensureAgentAssistantMemory.cjs` as template)
3. Wire it into `scripts/migrateServerDB/docker.cjs` — require + call after `migrator.migrate()`
4. Copy it into `/app/` in the `Dockerfile` (next to `ensureAgentAssistantMemory.cjs`)

These idempotent `CREATE IF NOT EXISTS` queries are safe to run on every container startup, regardless of journal state.

**Fresh environments are NOT affected** — an empty DB runs all migrations in order. The safety net only matters for existing deployments that have been through multiple image upgrades.

## Naming Conventions (Critical)

**Always match existing patterns before inventing new names.**

- AI provider variables: `minimax_*`, `kimi_*`, `openai_*` — follow the provider's config file pattern
- DB columns: `camelCase` (Drizzle convention)
- React components: `PascalCase.tsx`
- Hooks: `use*.ts`
- Tests: `__tests__/` directories or `.test.ts` suffix

## Git & branches

For every **fix** or **feature**: **never** implement on `main`. If currently on `main`, create a `feat/<slug>` or `fix/<slug>` branch before editing; if already on a non-`main` branch, continue there by default even when the troubleshooting target changes before GA. Do not create another branch just because the next fix/feature touches a different area. After each completed and verified fix/feature, create local commits automatically in every affected Git repository, including a separate local `wiki/` commit when Wiki pages changed; never amend a completed change to include later work. **Implementation plans** (Plan mode / `CreatePlan`) must include a short **Git workflow** subsection plus the no-push/tag/release-without-explicit-approval rule. Full workflow, canary/GA, and Docker release: **`.cursor/rules/git-and-release.mdc`**.

## Documentation impact

Every implementation plan and non-trivial change must follow
**`.cursor/rules/documentation-policy.mdc`**. Plans must state whether OpenWiki,
the user-facing GitHub Wiki, both, or neither need updates, with target pages and
a reason. User-facing workflows, settings, controls, deployment, configuration,
diagnostics, and troubleshooting changes require a GitHub Wiki update when the
wiki is available. OpenWiki does not replace user-facing documentation.
Root README and GitHub Releases follow **`.cursor/rules/release-writing.mdc`**.

## Branding / fork cleanup — when clearing values or rebranding

### If you set a SOCIAL_URL, BRANDING_EMAIL, or any URL constant to `undefined`

You MUST grep for every consumer of that constant and guard it. `<Link href={undefined}>` causes a Next.js prerender crash (`Cannot destructure property 'auth' of 'e' as it is undefined`) that **type-checking will not catch** — only the Docker build will.

Guard patterns:

```typescript
// For single links:
{SOCIAL_URL.x && <Link href={SOCIAL_URL.x}>...</Link>}

// For arrays of link items:
items={[...].filter((item) => !!item.href)}

// For JSON-LD arrays:
'sameAs': [SOCIAL_URL.x, SOCIAL_URL.github].filter(Boolean),
```

Key consumer files to check when changing branding constants:
- `src/app/[variants]/(main)/changelog/features/Hero.tsx`
- `src/app/[variants]/(main)/settings/about/index.tsx`
- `src/features/Follow/index.tsx`
- `src/features/User/UserPanel/useMenu.tsx`
- `src/server/ld.ts`

### `const` ordering in url.ts — TDZ errors

`export const A = B;` where `B` is defined further down the file causes a TDZ (Temporal Dead Zone) `ReferenceError` at build time. Type-checking passes. Only Next.js build catches it. When adding/changing URL constants, place them **before** any other constant that references them.

### Version check service (`src/services/global.ts`)

During a fork or rebrand, `getLatestVersion` MUST point to your own release feed, NOT the upstream npm package. Change the `VERSION_URL` fetch target — this is easy to miss because it lives in the services layer, not in branding constants.

### Full rebrand checklist (beyond branding constants)

When forking or rebranding, these files are commonly missed:

| File | Why |
|------|-----|
| `src/services/global.ts` | `VERSION_URL` fetches from upstream npm |
| `src/server/metadata.ts` | Hardcoded fallback description string |
| `src/app/manifest.ts` | Hardcoded app description |
| `src/locales/default/metadata.ts` | Locale descriptions (source of truth) |
| `locales/{en-US,zh-CN}/metadata.json` | Locale descriptions (served JSON) |
| `packages/const/src/url.ts` | `PLUGINS_INDEX_URL`, `AGENTS_INDEX_GITHUB`, etc. |
| `src/envs/app.ts` | `ASSISTANT_INDEX_URL`, `PLUGINS_INDEX_URL` npm registries |

## Durable background generation (Claude-like)

Leave a topic is not Stop. Server Graphile jobs survive closing the tab;
browser-fallback tools (code interpreter, image-designer, Kagi, non-HTTP MCP)
keep running only while that tab stays open. Contract and pitfalls:
**`.cursor/rules/durable-background-generation.mdc`**. Lesson:
`openwiki/architecture/claude-like-background-generation.md`.

## MCP OAuth 2.1

For MCP plugins with OAuth authentication (auto-discovery, redirect_uri, token exchange, callback flow): **`.cursor/rules/mcp-oauth.mdc`**. The `redirect_uri` must always be `${window.location.origin}/oauth/mcp-callback` — the app's own callback URL.

## Provider model options (extendParams / gear menu)

Adding or changing **per-model** API options (new provider module, new toggles) must follow **`.cursor/rules/provider-model-options.mdc`** (docs-first, end-to-end mapping, one provider panel at a time).

Dedicated gear panels live under **`src/features/ChatInput/ActionBar/Model/providers/`** and are wired from **`src/features/ChatInput/ActionBar/Model/index.tsx`** (see table in that rule file). **`README.md`** summarizes user-facing behavior for the fork.

## GitHub Wiki — separate sub-repo (CRITICAL)

The GitHub wiki is a **completely separate git repository** from the main codebase:

| Property | Value |
|---|---|
| Repo URL | `git@github.com:lqdflying/chathub.wiki.git` (SSH) |
| Workspace path | `wiki/` (gitignored in main repo) |
| Default branch | **`master`** (NOT `main`) |

### Sanity check — always verify before any wiki git operation

```bash
cd wiki && git branch -avv
```

Must show `* master` and `remotes/origin/master` pointing to `.wiki.git`. If it shows `main` or main-repo branches like `docs/tally-v3-to-v4`, the clone is **broken** — `.git` is missing or invalid and git commands are falling through to the parent repo. Fix by re-cloning (see below).

### Working with wiki pages

Use `git -C wiki` from the workspace root (no need to `cd`):

```bash
git -C wiki status              # MUST show wiki repo state, not main repo
git -C wiki add <page>.md       # stage changes
git -C wiki commit -m "..."     # commit to wiki repo's master branch
git -C wiki fetch origin master # fetch latest
git -C wiki push origin master  # push to wiki remote
```

### Clone / re-clone if broken

```bash
# Clone fresh to /tmp (SSH)
git clone git@github.com:lqdflying/chathub.wiki.git /tmp/chathub-wiki

# Make edits in /tmp clone, commit, push
cd /tmp/chathub-wiki
# ... edits ...
git add . && git commit -m "..." && git push origin master

# Then replace workspace copy
mv /home/opc/chathub/wiki /home/opc/chathub/wiki-broken
cp -r /tmp/chathub-wiki /home/opc/chathub/wiki
```

### NEVER do these

- **Never `rm -rf wiki/.git`** — corrupts the sub-repo and causes "Device or resource busy" sandbox file locks that prevent any further git operations.
- **Never assume wiki branch is `main`** — it is `master`.
- **Never commit wiki files to the main repo** — `wiki/` is gitignored; wiki pages live in the separate wiki repo.
- **Never work on wiki from a non-wiki branch** — if `git branch` in `wiki/` shows anything other than `master`, the clone is broken.

### Wiki page conventions

- Pages are plain `.md` files (e.g. `Home.md`, `Authentication.md`).
- Filenames with hyphens (e.g. `Docker-Deployment-and-Upgrades.md`) render as space-separated page titles on GitHub.
- The wiki has its own commit history, independent of the main repo.
